1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,699
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,580
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,080
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,460
Today, our topic is thank you.

8
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,980
Saying thank you today, not tomorrow.

9
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:25,900
Ben, who did you thank today?

10
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:27,900
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:28,500
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:30,960
Today, I thanked two people.

13
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:34,800
The bus driver, and my wife, for the coffee.

14
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,160
But I have a bigger one.

15
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:41,080
There is a message I have wanted to send for years.

16
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:42,940
To my old boss.

17
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:44,960
I never sent it.

18
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:46,800
What about you, Mia?

19
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:49,940
I have a letter, Ben.

20
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:50,940
It is in my desk.

21
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,580
I wrote it two years ago.

22
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,640
It is for my old English teacher, Mrs.

23
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:56,040
Park.

24
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,920
She is the reason I can do this podcast.

25
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,920
She told me, at fifteen, "Your English is good.

26
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:05,820
Keep going." Nobody else said that.

27
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:11,600
"Keep going." Mia, that is a big sentence for a fifteen-year-old.

28
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:13,140
And the letter?

29
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,420
Two years in the desk?

30
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:17,340
Two years, Ben.

31
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:18,520
Two pages.

32
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:20,060
I wrote it to thank her.

33
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,840
And I never sent it.

34
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:23,660
Because it felt strange.

35
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:24,780
Too much.

36
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:30,800
Twenty years later, a letter from a student she probably does not remember.

37
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:33,620
And I think many people have a letter like that.

38
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:35,480
In a desk, or in their head.

39
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:38,940
A small letter that never goes.

40
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:45,480
"A letter in a desk, or in the head." Mia, that is our topic today.

41
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,540
So here is our plan.

42
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:52,970
In part one, we talk about the thank yous we did not send, and the ones we did.

43
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:59,900
In part two, we ask why we wait, and why the other side feels different.

44
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:03,800
And I have a true story for that.

45
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:08,039
In part three, we share simple ways to say thank you today.

46
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:09,800
Does that sound good?

47
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:12,120
That sounds good, Ben.

48
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,440
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

49
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:17,940
You will hear it again.

50
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:23,720
You can listen while you cook, while you walk, or while you think of one person who helped you.

51
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:25,360
Okay, Ben.

52
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:26,180
Part one.

53
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:27,320
Your boss.

54
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:29,020
What did you want to say to him?

55
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:31,860
He taught me how to work, Mia.

56
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,780
Twenty years ago.

57
00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:37,880
I was young and lazy, and he did not shout.

58
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:39,609
He showed me.

59
00:02:40,670 --> 00:02:42,020
And I never told him that.

60
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:45,420
But last year, I sent it.

61
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:46,730
By accident, almost.

62
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,810
I was tired and a little sad, and I wrote three lines.

63
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:54,370
"You taught me how to work.

64
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:56,950
I still use it every day.

65
00:02:57,850 --> 00:02:58,340
Thank you."

66
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:00,680
By accident, Ben?

67
00:03:01,220 --> 00:03:01,850
You sent it?

68
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:05,960
I want to hear this, because I have never sent one by accident.

69
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:07,200
What happened?

70
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:09,440
He called me, Mia.

71
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,060
The same evening.

72
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:18,540
And he said, "Ben, nobody has thanked me in twenty years." Twenty years.

73
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:20,880
He was almost crying.

74
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:26,109
Three lines, and a man who had been waiting twenty years for them.

75
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,860
Twenty years of waiting, Ben.

76
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:30,620
Did he say anything else?

77
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:34,120
What do people say when a thank you finally arrives?

78
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,680
He said, "I thought nobody noticed," Mia.

79
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:39,800
That is what people say.

80
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:46,540
Not "thank you for the letter." They say, "I thought nobody noticed." Every time.

81
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:49,060
The nurse, the teacher, the boss.

82
00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:52,180
They all thought nobody noticed.

83
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,180
And one letter tells them that somebody did.

84
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:58,660
Nobody thanked him in twenty years, Ben.

85
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:03,240
That is so sad, and it is so common.

86
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:06,500
I think most people who help us never hear about it.

87
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:07,280
The teacher.

88
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:07,820
The nurse.

89
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:08,600
The boss.

90
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:13,420
They do the thing, we go on with our lives, and they never know it worked.

91
00:04:14,450 --> 00:04:15,580
They never know it worked.

92
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:16,980
Yes, Mia.

93
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:18,080
Mrs.

94
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:20,760
Park does not know you make an English podcast.

95
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:24,010
She does not know that "keep going" went anywhere.

96
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:26,080
And the small ones?

97
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,750
Today, how many people did you thank?

98
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,190
Real thank yous, with looking.

99
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:33,490
Today?

100
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,060
Let me think, Ben.

101
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,440
The man at the coffee shop.

102
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:37,980
Fast, no looking.

103
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:39,920
My colleague, for the file.

104
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,040
By email, one word.

105
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:43,160
That is two.

106
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,740
And both were small in the bad way.

107
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:49,720
Small like "thanks" thrown over a shoulder.

108
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:51,580
Not small like a real thing.

109
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:54,060
Small in the bad way, Mia.

110
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:56,620
I know that thank you.

111
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:59,050
"Thanks," over the shoulder, already walking.

112
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,180
And people do real things for us all day.

113
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,010
So what is small in the good way?

114
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:08,460
A thank you with a thing in it, Ben.

115
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:15,360
"Thank you for the file, I needed it before the meeting." That is small, and it is real.

116
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:21,060
It says, "I saw what you did." And I think that is what people want, more than the word.

117
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:22,460
To be seen.

118
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:26,820
"To be seen." Mia, that is the whole thing.

119
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:31,340
My friend who drove three hours when my car broke does not need a letter.

120
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,140
He needs me to say, "You drove three hours.

121
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:39,100
I have not forgotten." That is the whole letter.

122
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:43,340
"I have not forgotten" is the whole letter, Ben.

123
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:45,339
And I have more than one letter.

124
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:47,180
There is a note for a nurse.

125
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:48,720
A message for a neighbor.

126
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:51,400
All in my head or in my desk.

127
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:52,560
Not sent.

128
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:54,820
And the reason is always the same.

129
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,980
I think it will be strange for those people.

130
00:05:58,080 --> 00:05:58,740
Too much.

131
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:01,200
Too much, Mia.

132
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:02,790
I say the same.

133
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:05,100
And I still do not send mine.

134
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:06,880
So we are the same.

135
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:09,580
Okay, so that was part one.

136
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,500
The letters we did not send.

137
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:16,320
A teacher, a boss, a nurse, a friend with a car.

138
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:20,400
And one message that went by accident, and a man who waited twenty years.

139
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:23,000
Now, part two.

140
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:23,820
Why do we wait?

141
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:27,330
And I have a true story about exactly this.

142
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:30,600
About exactly this, Ben?

143
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:33,550
People who do not send their thank yous?

144
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,650
I did not know science looked inside desks.

145
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:38,660
Exactly that, Mia.

146
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:40,620
Okay, part two.

147
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:43,179
This is a true story.

148
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:48,620
Two researchers in Chicago, Amit Kumar and Nicholas Epley, did a study in 2018.

149
00:06:49,230 --> 00:06:53,220
They asked people to write a thank-you letter to someone who had helped them.

150
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,540
A real letter, to a real person who had helped them.

151
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:57,840
A real letter, Ben.

152
00:06:58,270 --> 00:07:00,260
Like mine, but they actually sent it.

153
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:01,720
And then?

154
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:02,900
What did the researchers do?

155
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:06,300
Before sending it, the writers had to guess, Mia.

156
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:08,300
How will the person feel?

157
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:09,380
How happy?

158
00:07:09,979 --> 00:07:10,740
How awkward?

159
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,520
And then the researchers sent the letters, and asked the receivers the same questions.

160
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:16,900
How did you feel?

161
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:18,100
Was it awkward?

162
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:20,860
And the two answers did not match.

163
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:22,780
How did they not match, Ben?

164
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:25,800
Did the receivers feel less than the writers thought?

165
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,300
Or more?

166
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:28,040
More, Mia.

167
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:29,540
The writers guessed too low.

168
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:30,760
Every time.

169
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:33,760
They thought the receiver would feel a little happy.

170
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:36,280
The receiver felt very happy.

171
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,040
They thought the letter would be a bit awkward.

172
00:07:39,629 --> 00:07:42,400
The receiver did not find it awkward at all.

173
00:07:42,459 --> 00:07:44,360
Not awkward at all, Ben?

174
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,220
But the writers were sure it would be awkward.

175
00:07:46,810 --> 00:07:48,350
That is my desk exactly.

176
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:49,940
I would have guessed the same.

177
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:52,940
And here is the part that explains your desk, Mia.

178
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:55,750
The writers worried a lot about the words.

179
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:57,200
Is it good enough?

180
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:58,620
Is it strange?

181
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:01,960
And the receivers did not care about the words at all.

182
00:08:02,710 --> 00:08:04,480
They only cared that somebody thought of them.

183
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:06,360
The words were small.

184
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,040
The thought was big.

185
00:08:08,630 --> 00:08:09,800
"The words were small.

186
00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:14,900
The thought was big." Ben, so the letter in my desk is not too much.

187
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:18,320
It is, actually, bigger than I think, for her.

188
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,440
And I am the one making it small.

189
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:23,060
You are the one making it small, Mia.

190
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:27,140
That is what the study found, with hundreds of people.

191
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:29,300
We think our thank you is smaller than it is.

192
00:08:29,950 --> 00:08:31,660
And we think it is stranger than it is.

193
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:33,420
Both wrong.

194
00:08:33,460 --> 00:08:34,740
Both in the same direction.

195
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:37,860
Both wrong, in the same direction.

196
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:41,920
So the fear says, "It will be strange." And the study says, "No.

197
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:47,460
It will be a very good day for that person." Ben, can I tell you about a thank you I received?

198
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:50,160
Because I think it shows the same thing.

199
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:50,719
Please, Mia.

200
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:51,710
Who thanked you?

201
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:54,860
I did not know you got one of these letters too.

202
00:08:54,870 --> 00:08:57,040
A student from my first English class, Ben.

203
00:08:57,600 --> 00:08:58,940
Five years ago.

204
00:08:59,100 --> 00:08:59,660
A short letter.

205
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:00,880
Two lines.

206
00:09:01,570 --> 00:09:03,000
"You said my accent was fine.

207
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,580
I stopped being afraid.

208
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:08,720
I work in London now." And I had no memory of saying it.

209
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:09,580
None.

210
00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:12,520
It was a small thing on a Tuesday.

211
00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:14,440
A small thing on a Tuesday, Mia.

212
00:09:15,050 --> 00:09:16,260
And it changed a life.

213
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:18,670
And he almost did not send it, I guess?

214
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:20,290
Because it felt too small?

215
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,000
He said exactly that, Ben.

216
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:31,320
"I almost did not send this." The biggest thing anyone has ever sent me, and he almost did not send it because it felt too small.

217
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:34,060
That is the study in one letter.

218
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:36,500
The study in one letter, Mia.

219
00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:39,900
And I think about all the people who did not send theirs.

220
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:42,160
All the letters in all the desks.

221
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:48,340
Small things on Tuesdays that changed lives, and the person who said them never knew.

222
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:52,500
That is a lot of good news, sitting in drawers.

223
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:54,960
A lot of good news, sitting in drawers.

224
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:56,910
Ben, why do we wait?

225
00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:59,380
Because I do not think it is laziness.

226
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:01,880
I think it is a kind of shyness, Mia.

227
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,920
Saying thank you, really, means showing that someone helped me.

228
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:07,810
That I needed help.

229
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:11,140
And some part of us does not want to be seen needing help.

230
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:14,700
So we say "thanks" fast and walk away.

231
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:18,400
The real thank you needs one second of standing still.

232
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:20,980
One second of standing still.

233
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:22,220
Yes, Ben.

234
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:25,260
And there is the word "today," in our title.

235
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:30,220
Not "say thank you." "Say thank you today." Why today?

236
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,720
Because thank you is the easiest thing in the world to move to tomorrow, Mia.

237
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:36,620
Nobody is waiting for it.

238
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:40,060
Nobody will be angry if it does not come.

239
00:10:40,070 --> 00:10:41,760
There is no deadline.

240
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:44,600
And that is exactly why it never comes.

241
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:47,620
People move it to tomorrow for twenty years.

242
00:10:47,660 --> 00:10:50,700
Today is the deadline, because nobody else will give you one.

243
00:10:51,750 --> 00:10:58,350
"Today is the deadline, because nobody else will give you one." Ben, that is true for every good thing, I think.

244
00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:00,630
No deadline, so no day.

245
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:03,200
Okay, so that was part two.

246
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:04,000
Why we wait.

247
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:07,180
We talked about Kumar and Epley and the letters.

248
00:11:07,710 --> 00:11:10,260
We said the words are small and the thought is big.

249
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:12,520
And we said today is the deadline.

250
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:14,720
Now, part three.

251
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:16,620
Simple ways to say thank you today.

252
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:18,640
Ben, you go first.

253
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:20,520
Okay, part three.

254
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:23,400
My first idea is the shape of the letter, Mia.

255
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,740
Because some people listening have a desk like yours, and I want to make the letter small.

256
00:11:28,780 --> 00:11:30,939
It does not need two pages.

257
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,220
Kumar and Epley's letters were short.

258
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,120
A few sentences.

259
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:35,720
Here is the shape.

260
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:37,860
One: what you did.

261
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:40,580
Two: what it changed for me.

262
00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:42,820
Three: thank you.

263
00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:45,100
Four: your name.

264
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:46,830
Four sentences.

265
00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:49,000
Four sentences, Ben.

266
00:11:49,510 --> 00:11:50,940
The two pages were the fear.

267
